  "worked_solution": "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives \nthe equation $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={210}^{∘},\\,{330}^{∘}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={90}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={210}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={270}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={330}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the product of the $x$-coordinates of $B$ and $D$ is \n\n$$\n210\\cdot 330=69\\,300.\n$$",
